====================================================================================================
v118  THE TUNNELING BARRIER AS A CIRCUIT:  what does thinness cost?
====================================================================================================
  n=8 qubits. w = (n - sum Z_i)/2, so diag(E(w)) = sum_k c_k * (all
  weight-k Z strings). Circuit cost = sum_k C(n,k) over nonzero c_k.
  TIER C for PART 1 (operator decomposition, no evolution) - sanctioned.

====================================================================================================
PART 1  MANY-BODYNESS OF THE BARRIER vs ITS WIDTH
====================================================================================================
  A thin feature in w is a high-frequency feature, so it should need large-k
  (many-body) Z terms. Wide barriers - exactly where v99 measured the walk
  LOSING - should be cheap. If that holds, the advantage is self-defeating.

   width   ||c||_1 by k (normalised, k=1..8)                     k90   gates
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     0.5   0.090 0.122 0.155 0.304 0.089 0.122 0.098 0.021     7     254
     1.0   0.194 0.095 0.292 0.264 0.034 0.098 0.021 0.002     6     246
     2.0   0.361 0.134 0.370 0.047 0.071 0.014 0.003 0.000     4     162
     4.0   0.311 0.452 0.187 0.036 0.013 0.001 0.000 0.000     3      92
     8.0   0.437 0.500 0.048 0.014 0.001 0.000 0.000 0.000     2      36

   k90 = body-order needed to capture 90%% of the potential's weight.
   gates = sum_{k<=k90} C(n,k), the rotations per Trotter step.

====================================================================================================
PART 2  TRANSMISSION vs TROTTER DEPTH   (TIER A: real circuit, shots)
====================================================================================================
  width=1.0, T=20.0, 16384 shots. Start |1..1> (w=n), measure P(|0..0>).
  'exact' is expm on the same H - the dense reference the circuit is
  checked against, which R1 sanctions.

    h     exact P(0)    reps=10     reps=40     reps=160    gates/step  depth
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     2.0   3.9383e-02   5.8716e-02  6.3354e-02  4.0833e-02      254  169442
     5.0   5.0634e-02   8.8501e-02  2.6489e-02  4.9011e-02      254  169442
    10.0   2.3834e-02   6.7200e-02  3.7903e-02  1.8250e-02      254  169442
    20.0   6.1107e-03   6.0547e-02  1.1780e-02  1.9592e-02      254  169442

====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  PART 1: THE PREDICTION HELD, AND MONOTONICALLY.

      width 8.0 (wide)  k90 = 2    36 gates/step   2-body, cheap
      width 4.0         k90 = 3    92
      width 2.0         k90 = 4   162
      width 1.0         k90 = 6   246
      width 0.5 (thin)  k90 = 7   254 gates/step   ~ALL 2^n Z strings

  254 of a possible 2^8 - 2 = 254. A width-0.5 barrier needs essentially
  EVERY Pauli-Z string on 8 qubits. The gate count is sum_{k<=k90} C(n,k):
  for a wide barrier k90=2 gives O(n^2); for a thin one k90 ~ n gives 2^n.

  SO THE MECHANISM IS SELF-LIMITING. A thin feature in Hamming weight is a
  high-frequency feature, and high frequency in w means MANY-BODY in Z. The
  thinness that lets the walk tunnel is exactly what makes the potential
  exponentially expensive to synthesise. And the wide barriers - cheap,
  2-body - are precisely where v99 measured the walk LOSING to descent.

  np.diag(E) hides all of it. That is the second time a dense matrix in this
  project concealed the dominant cost of the thing it was modelling; the
  first was v101's missing shot floor, worth 23x.

  PART 2: DEPTH IS PROHIBITIVE, TRANSMISSION IS NOT SETTLED.

  Depth 169,442 at reps=160 on EIGHT qubits. That is the honest cost of the
  circuit version, and it is far outside any NISQ coherence budget - the same
  wall that killed V5's QPE path at survival 0.098.

  But the transmission column does NOT settle v99's flatness claim, and this
  file should not be read as refuting it. Two reasons, both mine:
    - T is FIXED at 20 here; v99 maximised T over {5,10,20,40,80} per height,
      so the exact column is not the same quantity v99 reported.
    - n=8 against v99's n=20, and the barrier sits at w=n/4, so the geometry
      differs.
  The reps columns are also not converged - h=20 gives 0.0118 at reps=40 and
  0.0196 at reps=160, non-monotone, so 160 Trotter steps is still too few.

  WHAT SURVIVES: the many-body cost result in PART 1, which is exact,
  structural, and independent of the transmission question entirely.

  Scope: n=8 (v99 used 20), one width for PART 2, one T, no noise model,
  no seed averaging. This is the first circuit version, not the last word.
